        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Records of the Grand Historian</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 110 BCE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>, Grand Historian at the Han court and generally credited as the author, who continued the work begun by his father Sima Tan</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">A history in literary Chinese of about 526,500 characters in 130 chapters, in five sections: Basic Annals, Tables, Treatises, Hereditary Houses and Ranked Biographies; first written on bamboo slips</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val">Only two fragments of pre-Tang <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> survive, both at the Ishiyama-dera temple in Ōtsu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>; portions of nine Tang manuscripts are at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> and in Japanese temples and museums; read today in printed editions</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">The model for later dynastic histories; the Ranked Biographies; a span from the Yellow Emperor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a>; use of eyewitness accounts; a rich source of chengyu idioms; the biographies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a></span></div>
