    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> continued a history planned by his father Sima Tan and carried it from the legendary Yellow Emperor to the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a>, in about 526,500 characters.</li><li>Instead of one continuous narrative, he broke history into annals, tables, treatises, hereditary houses and biographies, a plan that set the model for all later dynastic histories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</li><li>The text was hidden for about twenty years after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>'s death, lost ten chapters and gained additions from other hands, and its earliest surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> pieces are held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Shiji was written on bamboo slips of 24 to 36 characters each. Endymion Wilkinson calculates that the whole work would have made between 466 and 700 bundles weighing 40 to 60 kilograms.</p></aside>
    </section>

